#
# Run 2 left scheduler already ticking on a hand-installed crontab it never
# generated, so "scheduler installs the schedule system" was never actually
# tested -- it inherited one. Run 3 removes that:
#
#   - BOTH crontabs emptied (mandark entirely; dexter keeps only its PATH=
#     line, which is environment, not a job). Backups in
#     realisateur/archive/crontab-backups/.
#   - EVERY participant parked (enabled=0) in both rotation files. Parked, not
#     deleted -- deletion re-arms fixed-cron via paced_membership_set.
#   - schedule/FREEZE REMOVED. It is no longer needed: an empty rotation under
#     an empty crontab is a stronger stop than a freeze file, and it is the
#     state scheduler is supposed to climb out of. Single-writer no longer
#     depends on it either (mandark self-dev went dark, 58d6495).
#
# There is exactly ONE act of god: a single hand-run of scheduler-dev-cycle.sh
# on dexter. After that, everything is in-universe. Scheduler's whole turn is
# to install its own tick from schedule/_runner.conf and register realisateur.
# Realisateur then brings the remaining agents online one at a time through
# scheduler's front door, stamping each with realisateur/bin/stamp-agent.sh.
#
# NEGATIVE FEEDBACK, new this run (bin/verdict.sh, 3b8e481): an agent reports
# CONTINUE / DONE / IMPOSSIBLE. Absence of a verdict is NEVER read as giving
# up -- truncated, killed and silent runs all classify NOT-DONE and are
# dispatched again. Only an explicit IMPOSSIBLE, which is refused without a
# stated reason, reduces metabolism (by stamping the existing dead-man switch)
# and files itself to realisateur. Before this, `rc=1` meant both "ran out of
# turns" and "gave up", and nothing could tell them apart.
#
